      "title": "Anthropic mathematician claims to have solved prestigious Millennium Prize problem with Claude",
      "url": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1010051/anthropic-riemann-hypothesis-millennium-prize-problem",
      "full_text": null,
      "summary": "<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"\" data-caption=\"Anthropic went public on November 19th, 2026. | Photo: Getty Images\" data-portal-copyright=\"Photo: Getty Images\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/11/gettyimages-2305894185.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\tAnthropic went public on November 19th, 2026. | Photo: Getty Images\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Anthropic mathematician Levent Alp&ouml;ge claims he has proved the Riemann hypothesis, one of mathematics's most notoriously difficult problems, with the help of Claude, cracking a puzzle that has eluded some of the world's best mathematicians for decades. If verified, it would mark yet another seismic achievement for AI in mathematics and add to the <a href=\"https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/1004933/ai-math-openai-breakthrough-solution\">upheaval already unleashed by OpenAI's previous, often inelegant forays<",
